## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a containerized TLS gateway with per-tenant isolation and real-time traffic policing, plus zero-downtime upgrades and firewall hardening. It also probes integration of Podman, Nginx, SNI routing, mTLS to backends, and practical rollback plans.

## Key Concepts
- Podman and Nginx TLS termination with SNI routing
- mTLS backends and backend certificate verification
- Per-tenant rate limiting (limit_req_zone or Lua scripting)
- Dynamic config reloads via systemd drop-in for zero-downtime upgrades
- nftables rules for inbound 8443 and outbound to backends
- Concrete test plans for bursts and rollback

## Code Example
```nginx
# nginx.conf (excerpt)
http {
  limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=tenantA:10m rate=10r/s;
  limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=tenantB:10m rate=5r/s;
  server {
    listen 8443 ssl;
    server_name tenantA.example;
    ssl_certificate /path/a.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /path/a.key;
    location / {
      limit_req zone=tenantA burst=100 nodelay;
      proxy_pass https://10.0.2.50:8080;
      proxy_ssl_server_name on;
      proxy_ssl_verify on;
    }
  }
  server {
    listen 8443 ssl;
    server_name tenantB.example;
    location / {
      limit_req zone=tenantB burst=50;
      proxy_pass https://10.0.2.51:8080;
      proxy_ssl_server_name on;
      proxy_ssl_verify on;
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale tenants beyond two while preserving fairness? 
- What changes for a third backend or different TLS ciphers? 
- How would you monitor and alert on rate-limit breaches and reload status?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical mastery of containerized TLS termination, lifecycle upgrades, and host firewall controls in RH9.

## Key Concepts
- Podman/nginx-like TLS termination
- systemd drop-ins for zero-downtime reloads
- nftables rate limiting
- SELinux policy for container access
- certificate rotation with hot reload

## Code Example
```bash
# container run (example)
podman run -d --name proxy \
  -v /srv/certs:/etc/nginx/certs:ro \
  -p 8443:8443:Z nginx:latest
```

```bash
# systemd drop-in (ExecReload for zero downtime)
[Service]
ExecReload=/bin/kill -HUP $MAINPID
```

```bash
# minimal SELinux module (conceptual)
module proxytls 1.0;
require {
  type container_t;
  class file read; class tcp_socket name_bind; 
}
# allow container_t to read certs and bind 8443
allow container_t cert_dir_t:file read;
allow container_t self:tcp_socket name_bind;
```

```bash
# nftables (inbound 8443 and global rate limit)
table inet proxy {
  chain input {
    type filter hook input priority 0;
    tcp dport 8443 accept
  }
}
table inet limit {
  chain input {
    type filter hook input priority 0;
    limit rate 100/minutediffers
  }
}
```

```bash
# test plan (rotation and rollback)
# 1. load test 100 req/min against https://host:8443
# 2. replace files in /srv/certs/rotated with new certs
# 3. touch a trigger or run systemd reload to apply without downtime
# 4. simulate revocation by replacing with old cert and reloading
```
